       process‐keyring - per‐process shared keyring

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  process  keyring  is  a keyring used to anchor keys on behalf of a
       process.  It is created only when a process requests it.   The  process
       keyring has the name (description) ___p_i_d.

       A  special  serial  number  value, KKEEYY__SSPPEECC__PPRROOCCEESSSS__KKEEYYRRIINNGG, is defined
       that can be used in lieu of the actual serial  number  of  the  calling
       process’s process keyring.

       From  the  kkeeyyccttll(1) utility, ’@@pp’ can be used instead of a numeric key
       ID in much the same way, but since kkeeyyccttll(1) is  a  program  run  after
       forking, this is of no utility.

       A  thread  created  using  the  cclloonnee(2) CCLLOONNEE__TTHHRREEAADD flag has the same
       process keyring as the caller of cclloonnee(2).  When a new process is  cre‐
       ated  using  ffoorrkk()  it  initially has no process keyring.  A process’s
       process keyring is cleared on eexxeeccvvee(2).  The process  keyring  is  de‐
       stroyed when the last thread that refers to it terminates.

       If  a  process doesn’t have a process keyring when it is accessed, then
       the process keyring will be created if the keyring is to  be  modified;
       otherwise, the error EENNOOKKEEYY results.

